==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Seijuro Hiko CC''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
Seijuro Hiko is very simple: he stays in the back and dishes out ludicrously high DPS, reaching 249,900 damage and ~35,197 DPS at Lv.30 (or 396,890 damage and 55,900 DPS at Lv.50). It goes without saying that these numbers are phenomenal, and he outranges a majority of basic targets with relative ease, even possessing [[Barrier Breaker]] for Starred Aliens. The Floating trait in particular is loaded with high-pressure melees that sometimes boast health ranges in the multi-millions, making Seijuro a perfect candidate for ripping huge chunks off their lifespan. With his [[Freeze]]'s uptime at ~33%, he can supercede the need for a dedicated crowd-controller entirely depending on the situation at hand. He's particularly good for [[Into the Future]] and [[Cats of the Cosmos]], where he can render many Alien roadblocks like [[Raging Bahamut Cat]] basically worthless.

However, Seijuro is not without his faults. He is highly susceptible to pierce of any kind, and has almost no resistances to speak of outside of [[Immune to Weaken]] and [[Immune to Slow|Slow]]—good for [[Calamary]], as well as [[Queen B]]/[[Croakley]] respectively. While he's not squishy, his knockback count allows him no opportunity to leverage his bulk against most Wavers or Surgers either, so he gets juggled to death relatively easily if you're not careful. Furthermore, his lack of pierce stands out given his mediocre foreswing animation; just like [[Shining Amaterasu (Uber Rare Cat)|Shining Amaterasu]], he actually has to hit an enemy for his high burst damage to matter. Seijuro attacks fast enough that it isn't too big of a problem, but he still won't be winning any gold medals for getting thwarted by an inconspicuously placed [[Gabriel]]. Regardless, these issues usually amount to nothing more than tedium, especially since you can get your value's worth out of him in just a few good swings. Consider yourself lucky if you end up with him.

